WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.792
Time-based logic can become difficult to teach and difficult to validate when it depends on the current date.

00:00:06.792 --> 00:00:14.332
Imagine a query that asks for admissions from the last thirty days using today's system date.

00:00:14.332 --> 00:00:20.462
If you run it this week and a student runs it next month, the result changes.

00:00:20.462 --> 00:00:24.027
That makes demonstrations, grading, and debugging harder.

00:00:24.027 --> 00:00:29.387
For this module, we use a fixed reference date: August 11, 2026.

00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:33.622
The thirty-day window is calculated relative to that fixed point.

00:00:33.622 --> 00:00:42.462
The query then keeps selected respiratory diagnoses and uses CASE to identify higher-risk cases when age is at

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:48.272
least sixty. This idea of a fixed “as-of date” is very useful in real analytics too.

00:00:48.272 --> 00:00:53.102
Financial reports often say “as of December 31.” A monthly retention report may evaluate customers as of the

00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:56.142
last day of the month.

00:00:56.142 --> 00:00:58.692
A clinical study may use a fixed index date.

00:00:58.692 --> 00:01:08.572
Machine-learning training data often needs an explicit cutoff date to prevent future information from leaking

00:01:08.572 --> 00:01:08.912
backward.

00:01:08.912 --> 00:01:15.607
Reproducibility means someone can rerun the same logic later and get the same answer from the same data.

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:22.452
So when time matters, distinguish between “right now” and “as of this defined reporting date.” Both are

00:01:22.452 --> 00:01:25.758
useful, but they solve different problems.
